  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "I don't really like making decisions. Every time I'm faced with something important, my first instinct is to delay it, just push it off for later. And honestly, I think that ends up being worse than making the wrong choice, because when I avoid deciding, it's like I'm carrying this little weight around. It's always in the back of my mind, kind of stressing me out. And then, when I finally make a choice, I have this habit of overthinking it. I replay it in my head, asking myself, \"Was that really the right move?\" I'm rarely 100% satisfied afterward. I mean, sometimes I am, especially when one option turns out to be clearly better than the other. But most of the time, I'm left kind of second-guessing myself. So lately, I've been thinking maybe the best thing I can do is keep it simple. Make a list of pros and cons, look at it on paper, and then just decide. And once I decide, stick with it without going in circles, because honestly, no decision is perfect and waiting forever doesn't really make life easier. At least when I choose, I move forward. So yeah, that's what I'm practicing, trusting myself more, letting go of perfection, and kind of sticking with whatever I choose.",
      "start_time": 0.119,
      "end_time": 75.04,
